math.h for bcc09's libc
=======================

These are from Sun's fdlibm ("freely distributable libm"), version 5.3
(2004), as https://netlib.org/fdlibm/ has it: each file keeps Sun's
notice, under which it may be used, copied, changed and distributed
freely.  bcc09's changes to them are under the GNU Library General
Public License, version 2, like the rest of libc (see COPYING.LIB).

fdlibm's algorithms work out each result with IEEE double arithmetic, one
correctly rounded step at a time, which libc's floating point helpers
(libc/bcc/fp*.s) do exactly.  So these give, bit for bit, what the same
code gives on any IEEE host: tests/src/mtest.c checks that against a
build of these files on the host.  The results are within about an ulp
of the exact values (fdlibm's own claim; glibc's differ from them by an
ulp or two now and then).

The C89 functions: acos, asin, atan, atan2, cos, sin, tan, cosh, sinh,
tanh, exp, frexp, ldexp, log, log10, modf, pow, sqrt, ceil, fabs, floor
and fmod; and copysign, scalbn, expm1 and finite, which some of them use.
HUGE_VAL is __huge_val (huge.s), an infinity: bcc09 can't make one from a
constant expression.  Each function is a module of its own, so a program
carries only what it uses (sin and cos bring the reduction of huge
arguments, k_rem_pio2.c, about 5K of code).

The changes:

- fdlibm wants a 32-bit int (for the halves of a double); bcc09's int
  has 16 bits.  Every int and unsigned is __i32 or __u32 (fdlibm.h: long
  on bcc09, int on a host), but for the C interfaces (frexp's int *,
  ldexp's and scalbn's int, finite's result) and a few small parameters
  of fdlibm's own (__kernel_sin's and __kernel_tan's iy,
  __kernel_rem_pio2's prec).  bcc09 doesn't convert arguments to a
  prototype's types (see BUGS), so a call that passes an __i32 to one of
  those casts it to int; and cc1 can't switch on a long, so those switches
  cast to int.

- __HI(x) and __LO(x), the high and low words of the double x, are the
  first and second on the 6809, which is big-endian (fdlibm.h).

- __ieee754_x is x (there are no wrappers: w_*.c and their
  _LIB_VERSION and matherr are left out), and __ieee754_rem_pio2 is
  __rem_pio2.  fdlibm.h replaces fdlibm's, declaring only what is used.

- errno is set as the GNU C library sets it: EDOM for acos and asin of
  more than 1 in size, log and log10 of a negative number, sqrt of one
  (or of -inf), sin, cos and tan of an infinity, fmod(x, 0) and
  fmod(inf, y), and pow of a negative number to a power that isn't an
  integer; ERANGE for log(0) and log10(0), and for the overflows of exp,
  expm1, cosh, sinh, pow and ldexp, and the underflows to 0 of exp, pow
  and ldexp (fdlibm's ldexp already did).  Not for a NaN argument.
  pow is a wrapper of fdlibm's (fdpow) that checks its result, as
  glibc's does.

- modf of a NaN gives a NaN, as C99 says (fdlibm gave 0).
